rites simple but clear texts describing or reviewing places to visit in the region, using an appropriate range of vocabulary and grammatical structures."}, {"type": "secondary_7", "content": " Writes simple but clear texts describing or reviewing places to visit in the region, using an appropriate range of vocabulary and grammatical structures."}, {"type": "secondary_8", "content": "Communicates effectively in English about the communicative topics for this level through the use of appropriate basic vocabulary and grammatical structures, and with intelligible pronunciation."}]}, {"type": "learning_points", "contents": [{"type": "heading", "content": " Simple Sentences"}, {"type": "describer", "content": "A simple sentence is a basic sentence with a subject and a verb. It can also have an object."}, {"type": "example", "content": "Sally went to the movies.<br /> I just purchased my ticket.<br /> I went to the airport."}, {"type": "text", "content": "A simple sentence:<br />   <ul><li>is a group of words expressing one idea   </li> <li>begins with a capital letter and ends with a period (.)</li>  <li>may be short or long</li> <li>must have a subject and a verb</li> <li>the subject goes before the verb</li> <li>can have more than one noun as the subject can use adjectives, adverbs, and prepositions to give more information </li></ul>"}, {"type": "text", "content": "Simple Past tense verbs can be regular or irregular.</br>      Regular verbs end in -ed or -ied.   </br>   Irregular verbs change in different ways.  </br>  </br>  For questions in the past, you use the auxiliary did followed by      the verb in the base form, like Did you ask \u2026 ? </br>     Did is the past form of to do. "}]}, {"type": "target_word", "contents": [{"type": "name", "content": "to surf"}, {"type": "definition", "content": "to stand on a board and ride the waves in the ocean  "}, {"type": "part_speech", "content": "(verb)"}, {"type": "example", "content": "Example: I want to learn how to surf."}, {"type": "audio", "content": "engenn3le08ob04aud01"}]}, {"type": "target_word_describer", "contents": [{"type": "text", "content": " These are words you use when you are going on a trip."}]}, {"type": "target_word", "contents": [{"type": "name", "content": "turtle "}, {"type": "definition", "content": "an animal with a hard shell on its back"}, {"type": "part_speech", "content": "(noun)"}, {"type": "example", "content": "Example: Sally wants to swim with the turtles."}, {"type": "audio", "content": "engenn3le08ob04aud02"}]}, {"type": "target_word", "contents": [{"type": "name", "content": "to purchase "}, {"type": "definition", "content": "to buy "}, {"type": "part_speech", "content": "(verb)"}, {"type": "example", "content": "Example: Sally purchased her plane ticket on Saturday."}, {"type": "audio", "content": "engenn3le08ob04aud03"}]}, {"type": "target_word", "contents": [{"type": "name", "content": "term "}, {"type": "definition", "content": "a period of time during the school year "}, {"type": "part_speech", "content": "(noun)"}, {"type": "example", "content": "Example: Peter wants to finish term one so he can go on holiday."}, {"type": "audio", "content": "engenn3le08ob04aud04"}]}, {"type": "target_word", "contents": [{"type": "name", "content": "to keep in touch "}, {"type": "definition", "content": "to stay in contact with someone "}, {"type": "part_speech", "content": "(expression)"}, {"type": "example", "content": "Example: We have to keep in touch when I am travelling."}, {"type": "audio", "content": "engenn3le08ob04aud05"}]}, {"type": "target_word", "contents": [{"type": "name", "content": "Champs-\u00c9lys\u00e9es"}, {"type": "definition", "content": "a famous avenue in Paris "}, {"type": "part_speech", "content": "(noun)"}, {"type": "example", "content": "Example: Let's walk down the Champs Elysees."}, {"type": "audio", "content": "engenn3le08ob04aud06"}]}, {"type": "background_image", "content": "engenn3le08ob04re1img01.png"}, {"type": "learning_points", "contents": [{"type": "heading", "content": "The ur sound"}, {"type": "describer", "content": "When a vowel is followed by an r, the r changes the sound that the vowel makes.  The vowel is called an r-controlled vowel. </br>The ER, IR, and UR Controlled R letter combinations produce the same er sound."}, {"type": "text", "content": " </br>There are 4 main ways of spelling this vowel sound.</br>Example: ur as in burger,  ir as in first,  ear as in earth,  er as in person  "}]}, {"type": "takeaway_extension", "contents": [{"type": "text", "content": "Choose a country you want to visit."}, {"type": "text", "content": " Make a list of 5 attractions you want to visit or activities you want to do."}, {"type": "text", "content": "Write 5 sentences about the attractions or activities."}, {"type": "text", "content": "Use three of these words: <span class=\"italic\">turn</span>, <span class=\"italic\">purchase</span>, <span class=\"italic\">surf</span>, <span class=\"italic\">Saturday</span>, <span class=\"italic\">church</span>."}, {"type": "text", "content": "Read the sentences aloud."}]}, {"type": "takeaway_assessment", "contents": [{"type": "text", "content": "I use words with the <span class=\"italic\">ur</span> sound."}, {"type": "text", "content": "I choose 5 attractions."}, {"type": "text", "content": "I write sentences about attractions or activities."}]}], "type": "sena"}